  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 202 PM MDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nSatellite imagery continues to show upper low along PacNW coast,\nslowing digging southeast. Southwest flow aloft remains in place\nover East Idaho, with warming temperatures for the next several\ndays. Stream of high clouds over Nevada/Utah poised to begin\nrotating slightly north overnight tonight. A few CAMs and even\nNBM picking up on weak potential for showers to develop late\ntonight across the Bear Lake region. Southerly flow aloft\ndeepens Wednesday into Thursday, allowing for better chances for\nshowers and thunderstorms to develop Wednesday afternoon,\nincreasing across the region Thursday. Even the high-res\nensembles on track with increase of thunderstorm potential\nThursday afternoon. Breezy south winds increase as well, which\ncould help push daytime highs warmer for some locations in the\nLower Snake Plain. Closed low ejects through Idaho Friday and\nFriday night. Models are picking up on development of dry slot\nduring the day across the Snake Plain Friday, limiting\nprecipitation chances to the higher elevations. As the low\npushes through Friday night, precipitation could wrap back\nacross the region before slowly shifting east with the low late\nin the day Saturday. Models and ensemble means follow the low\nwith development of a broad trough off the Pacific coast. East\nIdaho remains generally dry through the remainder of the\nforecast period, with relatively stable temperatures - daytime\nhighs in the lower to mid 70s and overnight lows in the upper\n30s to lower 40s.",
